---
import DocsLayout from '../components/DocsLayout.astro'
import DocSection from '../components/DocSection.astro'
import GridDiagram from '../components/GridDiagram.astro'
---

<DocsLayout
  current="/patterns/"
  eyebrow="Patterns"
  heading="The pages, composed."
  lede="The components are vocabulary. These are the sentences: the shell, the archive, the article, the shelf, the listening list."
>
  <div class="doc-copy">
    <p>
      A page pattern is a decision about where the eye should start and where
      it should rest. It is not a template with slots; it is a small set of
      rules that keep the page from inventing a new layout.
    </p>
  </div>

  <DocSection
    index="Pattern 00"
    title="The shell"
    lede="Every page starts on the same white page, in the same 1200px shell, with the same gutters."
  >
    <div class="doc-grid-2">
      <div class="doc-copy">
        <p>
          The header, main content and footer all consume
          <code>--z-layout-shell</code> and <code>--z-layout-gutter</code>.
          Nothing else sets a page width. The article uses the same shell with a
          centered 78ch measure at 22px; the margins are air, not a column.
        </p>
        <p>
          Gutters collapse from 32px to 24px to 16px as the viewport narrows.
          The page padding follows the layout, not the component.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="z-table-wrap" style="margin-top: 0">
          <table class="z-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Range</th><th>Shell</th><th class="text-right tabular-nums">Gutter</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>≥ 900px</td><td>1200px</td><td class="text-right tabular-nums">32px</td></tr>
              <tr><td>600–899px</td><td>fluid</td><td class="text-right tabular-nums">24px</td></tr>
              <tr><td>&lt; 600px</td><td>fluid</td><td class="text-right tabular-nums">16px</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection
    index="Pattern 01"
    title="The archive"
    lede="One centered column that begins with the list. No introduction panel, no sticky card, no sidebar."
  >
    <div class="doc-wire doc-wire--archive">
      <div class="doc-wire__air"></div>
      <div class="doc-wire__cell">Centered measure<br />Flat list · full dates</div>
      <div class="doc-wire__air"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-list doc-mt">
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">01</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>The page starts with the first entry.</strong> The site header carries the identity and the navigation; the archive carries the work. There is no introduction block to scroll past.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">02</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>One flat list.</strong> No year groups, no month dividers. Every row carries its full date so the list can be scanned from any point.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">03</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>Serif for the writing, mono for the apparatus.</strong> The title and excerpt are the author's voice; the date, series and language stay in the mono column.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">04</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>No thumbnails.</strong> A list of writing is a list of titles. Images would make a calm archive look like a feed.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="doc-mt-sm"><a class="text-label-13 text-accent underline decoration-accent/40 underline-offset-[0.25em] hover:decoration-current" href="/demos/archive/">Open the archive demo →</a></p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection
    index="Pattern 02"
    title="The article"
    lede="A centered measure, no sidebars, and an index that only exists when you reach for it."
  >
    <GridDiagram />
    <div class="rule-list doc-mt">
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">01</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>The title block is the only display size.</strong> Title, lede, then the metadata row and a hairline; the body starts after one clean gap.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">02</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>Serif reads; mono annotates.</strong> Newsreader carries the title, lede and body at 22px in a 78ch measure. Plex Mono carries dates, section numbers, the edge index and code. Mono never renders prose.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">03</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>Headings are numbered in the margin.</strong> The number is small and quiet; below 1180px it moves above the heading rather than disappearing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">04</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>The index lives at the edge.</strong> A fixed stack of hairline ticks, one per section, current one accented. Hover or focus reveals the labels; the page itself never reserves a column for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">05</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>End with a way forward.</strong> Footnotes, then a series pager, then the footer. No related-post grid.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="doc-mt-sm"><a class="text-label-13 text-accent underline decoration-accent/40 underline-offset-[0.25em] hover:decoration-current" href="/demos/essay/">Open the essay demo →</a></p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection
    index="Pattern 03"
    title="The shelf"
    lede="Books are objects, not cards. One page, one grid, no completion metric."
  >
    <div class="doc-wire doc-wire--reading">
      <div class="doc-wire__cell">Cover</div>
      <div class="doc-wire__cell">Cover</div>
      <div class="doc-wire__cell">Cover</div>
      <div class="doc-wire__cell">Cover</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-list doc-mt">
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">01</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>Covers keep their ratio.</strong> 2:3, a hairline border, no shadow, no tilt. A grid of book covers is already visually rich.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">02</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>The page title, then the objects.</strong> No introduction paragraph and no "Books" section label. The covers explain themselves; a visually hidden h2 keeps the semantics.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">03</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>Collapse 6 → 4 → 2.</strong> The grid changes column count; the covers do not change their proportions. Titles are serif; no stars, no counts.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="doc-mt-sm"><a class="text-label-13 text-accent underline decoration-accent/40 underline-offset-[0.25em] hover:decoration-current" href="/demos/reading/">Open the reading demo →</a></p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection
    index="Pattern 04"
    title="The listening list"
    lede="A ledger, not a player. One row per episode, the source named, the exit clear."
  >
    <div class="doc-wire doc-wire--listening">
      <div class="doc-wire__cell">01</div>
      <div class="doc-wire__cell">Episode title</div>
      <div class="doc-wire__cell">↗</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-list doc-mt">
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">01</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>Index, title, source, arrow.</strong> The number is the listening order, the title is serif, the source and duration are mono. The arrow leaves the site; there is no fake player UI.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">02</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>One 68ch column.</strong> The listening list keeps the index measure; the article is 78ch. It is still reading, not the shelf's full width.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rule">
        <span class="rule__mark">03</span>
        <p class="rule__text"><strong>No section label.</strong> The page title says Listening; the rows are obviously episodes. A visually hidden h2 keeps the semantics.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="doc-mt-sm"><a class="text-label-13 text-accent underline decoration-accent/40 underline-offset-[0.25em] hover:decoration-current" href="/demos/listening/">Open the listening demo →</a></p>
  </DocSection>

  <DocSection
    index="Pattern 05"
    title="The documentation"
    lede="This site is a pattern too: a sticky contents column and a single column of specimens."
  >
    <div class="doc-copy">
      <p>
        Documentation is the one place where a left column is navigation rather
        than marginalia. It uses an 11rem column, the same 2px accent rule for
        the current page, and the same hairline section breaks. The content
        column is wider (48rem) because a specimen needs room to breathe, but it
        never becomes a dashboard.
      </p>
    </div>
  </DocSection>
</DocsLayout>
